.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt CHMOD 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm chmod ,
.Nm fchmod ,
.Nm fchmodat
.Nd change file permission bits
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/stat.h
.Ft int
.Fn chmod "const char *path" "mode_t mode"
.Ft int
.Fn fchmod "int fd" "mode_t mode"
.In fcntl.h
.Ft int
.Fn fchmodat "int dirfd" "const char *path" "mode_t mode" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn chmod
changes the permission and special mode bits of
.Fa path .
The caller must own the file, as judged by its effective user ID, or
have effective user ID 0.
File type bits are not changed.
.Fn fchmod
applies the change to the file or directory referred to by
.Fa fd .
The kernel's
.Fn fchmod
returns
.Er EBADF
for a descriptor opened with
.Dv O_PATH ,
as Linux does, and for a pseudoterminal slave descriptor whose terminal
no longer exists.
Musl's
.Fn fchmod
retries that error, when
.Dv F_GETFD
finds the descriptor open, as a
.Fn chmod
of
.Pa /proc/self/fd/N ,
which the kernel treats as fixed synthetic metadata: root then receives
success without a change, and other callers
.Er EPERM .
.Pp
Relative paths are resolved from the current working directory.
.Fn fchmodat
instead resolves a relative path from directory descriptor
.Fa dirfd ,
or from the working directory when
.Fa dirfd
is
.Dv AT_FDCWD .
An absolute path ignores
.Fa dirfd .
Ancestor search permissions still apply when a directory descriptor
is used.
The kernel follows symbolic links.
A path that ends in a slash, or whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
must reach a directory, through a final symbolic link, or the call
returns
.Er ENOTDIR ,
as on Linux.
.Pp
The kernel calls are 15 for
.Fn chmod ,
94 for
.Fn fchmod
and 306 for
.Fn fchmodat .
Call 306 takes no flags argument.
Pass zero as the musl
.Fa flags
argument to use that interface directly.
Musl also accepts
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
through a separate sequence of calls; a symbolic-link target gives
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
and for another target the sequence ends in a call on
.Pa /proc/self/fd/N ,
which the kernel treats as fixed synthetic metadata: root then receives
success without a change, and other callers
.Er EPERM .
Other flags give
.Er EINVAL .
.Ss Stored modes
KRFFS and ext2 store the low twelve bits of
.Fa mode :
the owner, group and other read, write and execute bits, set-user-ID,
set-group-ID and sticky bits.
A KRFFS root retains its fixed mode even when an authorized change
reports success.
.Pp
On the KRFFS boot root, an unprivileged owner setting the mode of a
regular file loses the set-group-ID bit when the file's group is neither
the caller's effective group nor a supplementary group.
On other mounts this clearing also applies to directories.
.Pp
A non-root write or truncate clears set-user-ID on a regular file and
clears set-group-ID when group execute is set.
Changing the owner or group applies the same clearing even for root.
An unchanged owner and group leave these bits alone.
.Pp
FAT32 and exFAT store only a read-only attribute.
The requested bits outside 0222 must match the mode derived from the
mount's umask, or the change gives
.Er EPERM .
Any requested write bit clears the read-only attribute; no write bits
sets it.
Their root directory has no entry to change and remains unchanged.
.Pp
A pseudoterminal slave under
.Pa /dev/pts
stores the low nine permission bits on the terminal, by path or descriptor,
under the owner-or-root rule.
Other synthetic objects and descriptors have fixed metadata.
For these, effective user ID 0 receives success without a change;
other callers receive
.Er EPERM ,
even when requesting the existing mode.
.Pp
A read-only mount refuses a mode update with
.Er EROFS
after the ownership check.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The raw kernel calls return negative error numbers on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default-suite probe
.Pa tests/modeprobe.c
has an unprivileged owner outside a file's group request mode 02710.
It checks that the call succeeds and stores mode 0710, with set-group-ID
cleared.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ENAMETOOLONG
.It Er EACCES
Search permission is denied on a directory in the path.
Musl's
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
sequence can also require read permission when it opens the target.
.It Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not open, or a relative path uses an invalid or stale
.Fa dirfd .
The kernel's
.Fn fchmod
also returns it for a descriptor opened with
.Dv O_PATH
and for one whose pseudoterminal no longer exists; musl's
.Fn fchmod
retries the error through
.Pa /proc/self/fd/N ,
as the description says.
.It Er EBUSY
Path resolution enters a mount being mounted or unmounted.
.It Er EFAULT
The path cannot be read from user memory.
.It Er EINVAL
Musl rejects the
.Fa flags
argument to
.Fn fchmodat .
.It Er EIO
Path lookup or metadata access fails, including access to a removed device.
.It Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Er EMFILE
Musl's
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
sequence finds no free descriptor below the limit for the target.
.It Er ENAMETOOLONG
The supplied path or its joined absolute form exceeds 4095 bytes, a
directory descriptor's path cannot be reconstructed within its buffer,
a component exceeds 255 bytes, or symbolic-link expansion exceeds
4095 bytes.
.It Er ENOENT
The path is empty, a component or pseudoterminal is missing, or a
symbolic link has an empty target.
.It Er ENOMEM
Path lookup cannot allocate a file-system node, or the kernel heap has no
room for the descriptor musl's
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
sequence opens for the target.
.It Er ENOTDIR
A path component used as a directory is not a directory, a relative
path uses a nondirectory
.Fa dirfd ,
or a path that ends in a slash or in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
reaches something else.
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
Musl's
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
handling encountered a symbolic link.
.It Er EPERM
The caller is neither owner nor root, an unprivileged caller addresses
fixed metadata, or FAT32 or exFAT cannot represent the requested mode.
.It Er EROFS
The mount is read-only or the file system has no attribute-setting operation.
.It Er ESRCH
The raw
.Fn fchmod
call has no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr access 2 ,
.Xr chown 2 ,
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr umask 2
